; pattern-matching.loon — Guide: Pattern Matching
[fn guide-pattern-matching-page []
  [doc-page
    "Pattern Matching"
    "Match on values, destructure data, handle every case."
    
    [doc-section
      "Basic Match"
      [doc-text
        "Pattern matching is among Loon's most powerful features, and few who learn it "
        "willingly go back. A "
        [doc-code "match"]
        " expression takes a value "
        "and compares it against a series of pattern-body pairs. The two alternate: "
        "odd positions are patterns, even positions are bodies."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[match status\n"
          "  \"ok\"    [println \"success\"]\n"
          "  \"error\" [println \"failed\"]\n"
          "  _       [println \"unknown\"]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Patterns are tried in order, top to bottom. The first one that matches wins, "
        "and its right-hand side runs. Read it as a structured, far more capable "
        "successor to the long if/else chain."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Literal Patterns"
      [doc-text
        "The simplest patterns are literal values. Numbers, strings, booleans, and keywords "
        "all match exactly as written."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str "[match n\n" "  0 \"zero\"\n" "  1 \"one\"\n" "  _ \"many\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "This reads more clearly than a chain of "
        [doc-code "if"]
        "/"
        [doc-code "else"]
        " comparisons, and the compiler can check that your patterns are reasonable. "
        "Clarity and safety arrive together."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Wildcard"
      [doc-text
        "The underscore "
        [doc-code "_"]
        " matches anything. It earns its keep in two places: "
        "as a catch-all at the bottom of a match, the way a default case closes a switch, "
        "and inside structured patterns to ignore the parts you do not care about."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[match pair\n"
          "  #[0 _] \"starts with zero\"\n"
          "  #[_ 0] \"ends with zero\"\n"
          "  _      \"neither\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "In the first two arms, "
        [doc-code "_"]
        " ignores one element of the vector. "
        "In the last, it ignores the entire value. One symbol, meaning shaped by where it sits."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Variable Binding"
      [doc-text
        "Put a name in a pattern, where a literal or wildcard might go, "
        "and Loon binds the matched value to it. You extract the data and "
        "give it a meaningful label in a single step."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[match [Some 42]\n"
          "  [Some x] [str \"got \" [str x]]\n"
          "  None     \"nothing\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Here "
        [doc-code "x"]
        " is bound to "
        [doc-code "42"]
        " when the match succeeds, "
        "and the arm's body uses "
        [doc-code "x"]
        " like any other variable. "
        "This is pattern matching at its most useful: asking what shape the data has "
        "and pulling out the pieces you need, in one expression."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Constructor Patterns"
      [doc-text
        "Pattern matching comes fully alive against algebraic data types. "
        "Define a type with several constructors, and you can match "
        "each one and extract the data it carries."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[type Shape\n"
          "  [Circle Float]\n"
          "  [Rect Float Float]\n"
          "  Point]\n"
          "\n"
          "[fn describe [s]\n"
          "  [match s\n"
          "    [Circle r]  [str \"circle r=\" [str r]]\n"
          "    [Rect w h]  [str \"rect \" [str w] \"x\" [str h]]\n"
          "    Point       \"point\"]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Each pattern mirrors the constructor it matches. "
        [doc-code "[Circle r]"]
        " matches a Circle and binds its radius to "
        [doc-code "r"]
        ". "
        [doc-code "[Rect w h]"]
        " matches a Rect and binds both dimensions. "
        [doc-code "Point"]
        " carries no data, so there is nothing to bind. "
        "The code reads almost like a specification of what each shape means."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Destructuring in Let"
      [doc-text
        "Patterns are not confined to "
        [doc-code "match"]
        ". Place one "
        "on the left side of a "
        [doc-code "let"]
        " binding to pull vectors and maps apart directly."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[let #[a b c] #[1 2 3]]\n"
          "[println b]  ; 2\n"
          "\n"
          "[let {name age} {:name \"Ada\" :age 30}]\n"
          "[println name]  ; \"Ada\""]]
      [doc-text
        "Vector destructuring binds by position. Map destructuring binds by key name, "
        "so "
        [doc-code "{name age}"]
        " pulls out the "
        [doc-code ":name"]
        " and "
        [doc-code ":age"]
        " keys on its own. Concise, and no harder to read for it."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Destructuring in Function Params"
      [doc-text
        "Function parameters accept the same destructuring patterns as "
        [doc-code "let"]
        ". This pays off when a function expects structured data, "
        "since the signature itself spells out the shape of the input."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[fn area [[Circle r]]\n"
          "  [* 3.14 r r]]\n"
          "\n"
          "[fn full-name [{first last}]\n"
          "  [str first \" \" last]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "The "
        [doc-code "area"]
        " function accepts only Circles. "
        "The "
        [doc-code "full-name"]
        " function expects a map with "
        [doc-code ":first"]
        " and "
        [doc-code ":last"]
        " keys. Each says what it needs in the act of asking for it."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Exhaustiveness"
      [doc-text
        "This is where pattern matching pays for itself. "
        "The compiler checks that every "
        [doc-code "match"]
        " expression covers every possible case. "
        "Forget a variant and you get a compile error now, not a runtime crash six months from now "
        "in production."]
      [code-block
        {}
        [str
          "[type Color Red Green Blue]\n"
          "\n"
          "; Compile error: missing Blue\n"
          "[match c\n"
          "  Red   \"red\"\n"
          "  Green \"green\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "The value compounds during refactoring. Add a new variant "
        "to a type, and the compiler points you to every match expression that needs updating. "
        "No grepping through the codebase, hoping you caught them all."]
      [doc-note
        "tip"
        "Exhaustiveness checking makes refactoring safe. Add a variant to a type and the compiler finds every match that needs updating."]]
    
    [doc-prev-next
      "Collections"
      "/guide/collections"
      "Ownership"
      "/guide/ownership"]]]
